First, I need to think of scenarios where a phone can't connect to the cloud. For example, if the phone is in an area with no internet, on-device AI would still work. That's a key point. So the first item could be about offline functionality.

Next, privacy. On-device AI doesn't send data to the cloud, so users can keep their data local. That's a big advantage. Then, real-time processing. Since the phone processes data locally, there's no delay, which is important for things like voice commands or instant translations.

Battery life is another factor. Cloud models require internet, which uses more power. On-device AI is more efficient, so better battery life. Also, data security. If the phone is on a public network, sending data to the cloud could be a risk. On-device processing keeps data safe.

